**Script:**
```javascript
var headers = new Array();
headers['User-Agent'] = 'Mozilla/5.0'; // or whatever

fetch('https://example.com', {
    method: 'GET',
    headers: headers // set the header from above
});

fetch('example.com', {
    method: 'GET',
    headers: headers // set the header from above
});
```

**Why it works:** The `User-Agent` header tells the browser which site is requesting a response. When you fetch two requests with the same header, they get treated as duplicates, causing the server to reject the second one.

If you're trying to block IP addresses, add `{ rejectUnauthorized: true }` in your headers. That will prevent any request from that IP from making it to the target server.

**Common pitfalls:** Don't use `fetch` without setting the headers — it won't enforce them. Also, `fetch` doesn't validate permissions; if it tries to fetch restricted pages, it'll fail silently.

If you're doing this for security reasons (access control), add `{ rejectUnauthorized: true }` alongside the headers so the server knows to reject the request.

